    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 231706\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1005 AM MST Wed Sep 23 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Chances for scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms are \nincreased today and Thursday with the most activity focused over \nfoothill and high terrain areas.\n\n- Along with the chances for rain, gusty storm driven outflow winds \nwill be possible today and Thursday, potentially leading to patchy \nblowing dust.\n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures will persist through \nthe weekend before additional cooling is expected early next week.\n \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Thursday/...\nBroad upper level troughing remains positioned over the Southwestern \nU.S. with several smaller scale disturbances within the larger scale \ntrough and abundant moisture from eastern Arizona through New \nMexico. The main forecast concerns for today and Thursday will be \nthe potential for isolated to scattered thunderstorms which will be \ncapable of producing strong downdraft and outflow winds, along with \nvery localized heavy rainfall. Current objective analysis shows \nlower level easterly flow increasing across southeast into south-\ncentral Arizona spreading moisture westward while water vapor \nimagery shows multiple disturbances south and east of our area. \n\nFor the rest of this morning and into the afternoon, the easterly \nflow is expected to increase moisture levels with low level mixing \nratios of 8-10 g/kg shifting into south-central Arizona. The \nforecast for this afternoon through early tonight remains somewhat \nuncertain as the main convective potential should be focused well to \nour east in New Mexico associated with disturbance moving north \nnortheastward through the state. For our area, we will fall under \nlarger scale ascent associated with the trough and a jet max to our \nnorth leaving the higher terrain as the best area of focus for \nshower and thunderstorm development this afternoon. \n\nGiven the available moisture and modest heating today under partly \nsunny skies, forecast MUCAPE should reach 1000 J/kg over the south-\ncentral Arizona lower deserts to 500-750 J/kg to our north and east. \nHi-res CAMs have been inconsistent with the amount of convection for \ntoday into tonight, but they have mostly kept with initiation over \nthe higher terrain later this afternoon. Given a somewhat dry \nenvironment in the lower deserts, DCAPEs of 1200-1500 J/kg are \nexpected which should lead to high chances of one more more outflows \nbeing generated. Even though the CAMs have been fairly inconsistent, \nstorm outflows are expected which in turn may be enough to initiate \nfurther storm development over the lower deserts this evening. These \nchances (20-30%) will be contingent upon at least one fairly strong \noutflow, or even colliding weaker/smaller outflows. Some iterations \nof the CAMs have even suggested a large strong outflow progressing \nas far west as La Paz and Yuma Counties, sparking brief showers and \nstorms late in the evening to as late as 2 AM tonight. \n\nThursday's forecast looks even murkier as moisture is expected to \ncontinue to spread westward through southwest Arizona into southeast \nCalifornia, while the upper level ascent is likely to remain \npositioned over the eastern half of Arizona. HREF shows even higher \nMUCAPEs for Thursday with the bulk of area reaching 1000-1250 J/kg \nduring the afternoon hours while an upper level shortwave is \nforecast to move through southeast Arizona. However, there seems to \nbe even more uncertainty within the models as the CAMs show little \nto no convection developing across southern Arizona with the \nmajority staying to our north. Given the higher instability and at \nleast some upper level ascent, it would seem we'd have at least \nsimilar chances as today, but NBM PoPs are mostly below 10% for the \nlower deserts. Forecast soundings do still show weak to possibly \nmodest convective inhibition over the lower deserts, so like today \nwe'd mostly have to rely on some sort of strong outflow or outflow \ncollisions for storms to develop over the lower deserts. Needless to \nsay, uncertainty remains quite high for Thursday's rain chances. If \nstorms actually do get going on Thursday, gusty winds and localized \nheavy rainfall will again be possible given the expected decent \ninstability and modest DCAPE.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Friday through Tuesday/... \nBy Friday, the main upper level trough is forecast to shift \nnortheastward out of a our region leaving dry southwesterly flow in \nplace. Guidance shows low level mixing ratios quickly dropping to 5-\n7 g/kg across the lower deserts to only 6-8 g/kg over the Arizona \nhigh terrain. As a result, Friday will begin another dry period with \nonly lingering chances for some isolated showers and maybe a few \nthunderstorms over the eastern Arizona high terrain. Temperatures \nare forecast to remain quite stable over the next few days with \nhighs right around to slightly above normal and lows a few degrees \nabove normal.\n\nWesterly dry flow should persist through at least Saturday with \nlarge scale troughing remaining to our northwest while the \nsubtropical ridge slowly shifts southwestward from east Texas into \nthe interior of Mexico. This shift in the ridge is likely to bring \nhigher heights temporarily for our region later Friday through \nSaturday, so daytime highs Saturday are likely to top out between \n100-104 degrees (4-6 degrees above normal) under mostly sunny skies.\n\nForecast uncertainty increases dramatically going into early next \nweek as it will likely depend upon what happens with Hurricane Polo, \nthe positioning of the subtropical ridge, and an upper level trough \nthat may swing through our region on or around next Monday. The \nlatest NHC track for Polo has it slowly making its way westward over \nthe next few days before turning more to the north this weekend, but \nstaying west of southern Baja. This track may be enough to help \nadvect tropical moisture northward as far as our area, but \nuncertainty remains quite high. There is a low end potential of \ntropical moisture making its way northward into our area just before \nthe aforementioned trough swings through our region and this could \nlead to chances for strong to severe storms along with heavy rain \npotential on or around Monday. The more likely scenario would \ninvolve modest moisture making its way into our area, but still with \nthe support of the trough. The trough may still be strong enough to \nsupport some strong to severe storms due to much higher than normal \nshear, but with less moisture it would lower the severe threat as \nwell as well as the threat for heavy rainfall. At this moment, we \nare unsure which solution will win out, but we expect to have a \nbetter idea by the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1705Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nVariable wind directions with the potential for gusty evening \noutflows followed by the threat of SHRA/TSRA will be main weather \nissues through Thursday afternoon under thick, high cirrus cigs and \nbrief periods of cigs as low as 8-10K ft AGL. Confidence is good \nthat E/SE winds will prevail into the mid/late afternoon before \nforecast confidence decreases. Wind directions may briefly go S/SW \nlate afternoon/early evening, but a 140v240 oscillation may be more \naccurate. \n\nConfidence is increasing that thunderstorms over the Rim will send a \nNE outflow with an abrupt wind shift into the PHX airspace early/mid \nevening (02-05Z timing) with gusts briefly reaching 30-35kt. A few \nSHRA/TSRA may impact the area, and have maintained a prob30 mention \nof TS, though confidence in development and coverage in the PHX \nmetro is very low. Confidence is better that once activity clears \nthe metro later this evening, east winds will hold over the area \nthrough much of the day Thursday. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo weather issues will exist through Thursday afternoon under \nincreasing high cirrus decks. Confidence in a specific wind \ndirection at any given time is low given the weak pressure gradient. \nExtended periods of light and variable winds will be common. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nToday into tonight will bring modest chances 30-50% for showers and \nthunderstorms, focused over the eastern districts, while the western \ndistricts should mostly remain dry. Gusty outflow winds with abrupt \nwind shifts are expected during the first part of the evening. \nMinRHs will fall into a 15-20% range today for the western districts \nand 20-25% Thursday, while the eastern districts will fall to 25-40% \ntoday and Thursday. Winds across eastern districts will generally \nmaintain a east southeast direction, frequently becoming gusty while \nlighter, diurnally driven winds will persist in western districts.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
